  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current approach uses a grid of increasing n (47, 94, 188, 376) with multiple starting points and local optimization methods (SLSQP and COBYLA) to minimize the maximum overlap integral. However, this method is prone to local optima and does not scale well to larger n. The central idea is to use a genetic algorithm with adaptive repair mechanisms and a multi-start local search to escape local optima. This approach is chosen because:\n1. Genetic algorithms are better at exploring the search space and finding global optima\n2. The problem is non-convex and requires escaping local minima\n3. The objective function is expensive to evaluate, so efficient exploration is critical\n4. The solution must satisfy box constraints and a linear equality constraint\n\nThe bottleneck addressed is the inability of local optimization methods to find the global minimum, especially when starting from the provided initial solution. The genetic algorithm will be used to explore the search space, followed by local optimization to refine promising candidates.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: h is represented as a vector of length n_points in [0,1] with sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Objective: minimize max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx (discrete approximation)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, and sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Optimization: genetic algorithm with tournament selection, uniform crossover, and adaptive mutation\n- Repair: post-crossover mutation repair to maintain constraints\n- Local search: SLSQP and COBYLA for each generation's best candidate\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize population using provided initial_h_values and random generation\n2. For each generation:\n   a. Evaluate fitness (maximum correlation) for each individual\n   b. Tournament selection with replacement\n   c. Apply uniform crossover with probability 0.9\n   d. Apply adaptive mutation with probability 0.1\n   e. Repair offspring to satisfy constraints\n   f. Local search (SLSQP/COBYLA) on the best candidate from crossover/mutation\n   g. Replace worst individuals with new offspring\n3. Track best solution across generations\n4. Terminate when time budget is exceeded or maximum generations reached\n5. Return best solution found\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Population size: 20-50 (starts at 20, increases if time remains)\n- Mutation rate: 0.1 (adjusts based on diversity)\n- Crossover rate: 0.9\n- Local search iterations: 100 (adjusts based on time)\n- Repair strategy: uniform scaling followed by clipping\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Dominant cost: fitness evaluations (O(n_points^2))\n- Estimation: 1000 evaluations per second for n=376\n- Schedule: 100 generations initially, adjusts based on time\n- Fallback: return best solution found if time expires\n- Instability: handle NaN/inf in objective by repair\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Check sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 and 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Re-evaluate final candidate with provided objective\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with c5_bound = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Ensure c5_bound is finite and computed correctly",
